# Specification

## Requirements

## Nexus Rendering Engine Polish — Terminal Progressive Enhancement

### Overview
Upgrade Nexus's TUI rendering layer to leverage modern terminal capabilities, delivering flicker-free rendering, universal clipboard support, adaptive color profiles, enhanced keyboard input, and subtle micro-transitions. This is a rendering-layer upgrade that wraps existing views — no core logic rewrites.

### Non-Negotiables
- Must remain compatible with existing session files (YAML config, SQLite store)
- Keep the current tech stack foundation (Go, Bubbletea, Lipgloss, Ebiten for GUI)
- No external service dependencies (everything local)
- Don't rewrite working core logic (models, Update loops, business logic stay intact)
- Don't break existing keybindings or user muscle memory

### Feature Requirements

#### R1: Synchronized Output (Mode 2026)
- Wrap all TUI render cycles in Mode 2026 escape sequences (`CSI ? 2026 h` to begin, `CSI ? 2026 l` to end)
- Detect terminal support via DECRPM query (`CSI ? 2026 $ p`) at startup
- If terminal supports Mode 2026, enable synchronized output automatically
- If terminal does not support it, fall back gracefully to current rendering behavior
- This eliminates micro-flicker on every frame, especially noticeable over SSH connections
- Must work correctly inside tmux (tmux supports Mode 2026 since 3.3a)

#### R2: OSC 52 Clipboard
- Implement OSC 52 escape sequence for clipboard read/write in the TUI
- Replace dependency on `github.com/atotto/clipboard` for TUI-side clipboard operations (yank commands, copy connection details, etc.)
- OSC 52 works over SSH, inside tmux, and in screen — no X11/Wayland required
- Detect OSC 52 support at startup; fall back to `atotto/clipboard` if not supported (e.g., very old terminals)
- The GUI side (Ebiten-based RDP/VNC) retains its current clipboard implementation unchanged
- Maximum clipboard payload: 100KB base64-encoded (per terminal convention)

#### R3: Adaptive Color Profiles
- Detect terminal color capability at startup: true color (24-bit) → 256 color → 16 color → monochrome
- Detect background luminance (light vs dark) via OSC 11 query where supported
- Auto-downsample the active theme's colors to the best available profile
- Existing theme system (TokyoNight Storm default, 8 built-in themes) remains unchanged — adaptive rendering is a presentation layer below the theme
- Users on a 256-color SSH session automatically get the closest-match rendering without manual configuration
- Add a config option `color_profile: auto | truecolor | 256 | 16 | mono` to override auto-detection
- Light/dark adaptive colors for themes that support it (existing themes are dark-only, but the infrastructure should support future light themes)

#### R4: Kitty Keyboard Protocol
- Detect Kitty keyboard protocol support at startup via progressive enhancement query
- When supported, enable enhanced key reporting to resolve ambiguous sequences:
  - Distinguish Escape key from Alt+key sequences (eliminates the mode-switch delay hack)
  - Enable Shift+Enter, Ctrl+Period, and other modified key combinations
  - Support key release events (useful for future hold-to-peek patterns)
- When not supported, fall back to current key handling (no regression)
- Do NOT change any existing keybindings — this only improves detection accuracy of existing bindings
- Expose new unambiguous key events to the existing keymap system so future keybindings can leverage them

#### R5: Micro-Transitions
- Subtle fade effect on view transitions (e.g., switching from list view to detail view): 5-8 frames via tea.Tick at ~16ms intervals (~80-130ms total)
- Smooth flash message appearance and auto-dismiss: fade in over 3 frames, hold, fade out over 5 frames
- Loading spinner during health checks and connection attempts with contextual status text
- All animations must be:
  - Under 200ms total duration (terminal users expect snappy, not cinematic)
  - Skippable — any keypress during animation jumps to the final state immediately
  - Disableable — config option `animations: true | false` (default true)
  - Implemented without blocking the Update loop (use tea.Cmd/tea.Tick)
- Do NOT animate frequently-repeated actions (scrolling, filtering) — only state transitions

### Technical Constraints
- All capability detection must use timeout-based queries (send escape sequence, wait ≤100ms for response, fall back to safe default if no response)
- Terminal capability detection runs once at startup and caches results
- Must not break rendering in terminals that don't support any of these features (PuTTY, basic xterm, Windows Console)
- Must work correctly over SSH (all features degrade gracefully)
- Must work correctly inside tmux and screen (tmux supports most of these; screen may not)
- Performance: capability detection must complete in under 500ms total at startup


## User Stories

As a sysadmin SSHing into a remote bastion, I want Nexus to render without flicker so that the interface feels solid and professional even over high-latency connections.

As a user running Nexus inside tmux over SSH, I want clipboard yank commands to actually reach my local clipboard so that I can copy connection details without leaving the terminal.

As a user with a 256-color terminal profile, I want Nexus to automatically render the best possible colors for my terminal so that I don't have to manually configure a degraded theme.

As a neovim user, I want Escape to register instantly without the ambiguity delay so that mode switching feels as fast as in my editor.

As a user navigating between views, I want subtle visual transitions so that context switches feel smooth rather than jarring, but I never want to feel like I'm waiting for an animation.

As a user on a minimal terminal (PuTTY, basic xterm), I want Nexus to work exactly as it does today so that none of these enhancements break my existing workflow.
